     4	> this file is its signing act** (the house form since Amendment 5). Drafted 2026-10-16 by the AI collaborator
     5	> (Claude, Opus 5.5, owner instance) from `docs/reviews/plain-pilot/CODEX-REVIEW.md` ("Findings about A9 itself",
     6	> A01–A06), A9, A8 §A8.3 and §A8.5, A2 §A2.1, A1 §A1.6, `docs/V1-PROFILE.md` §7.1–§7.2 and
     7	> `formal/p8/ENCODING-V1.md` §7. Revised 2026-10-16 after the Codex non-author review of this draft
     8	> (`docs/reviews/amendment-10/CODEX-REVIEW.md`), applying all eight findings as disposed in
     9	> `docs/reviews/amendment-10/DISPOSITION.md`. Layered on the original and Amendments 1–9. Per amend-don't-rewrite,
    10	> none of them is edited: every registered sentence this amendment changes is listed in §A10.7 with its source line
    11	> and its replacement, and is left standing in its file. **A10.4 is AWAITING AUTHOR RULING.** The author rules on
    12	> it in his own words before the signing commit; if he rejects or changes it, the owner revises A10.4 first.
    13	
    14	## In plain words
    15	
    16	This amendment corrects six wording problems in Amendment 9. It adds no mechanism. The verifier uses the same
    17	checks and gives the same verdicts. A review of the plain version of Amendment 9 found the six problems.
    18	
    19	Bitcoin is a public system that adds blocks to a chain. A block header states a chain time. This value is not a
    20	measurement of real time. The registered number k is six.
    21	
    22	- **A01.** Amendment 9 says that the content existed "no later than" T. T is a chain time, not real time. The
    23	  correction says this in the claim and in the other sentences that use T in this way.
    24	- **A02.** Amendment 9 says, in its own words, that the keys "were allowed to issue during a stated window". The
    25	  verifier makes only two comparisons with the window. The correction states only those two comparisons.
    26	- **A03.** Amendment 9 excludes Bitcoin history "deeper than k". The correction defines the depth of a
    27	  reorganization. The exclusion then also includes a reorganization of exactly k blocks. The models already
    28	  exclude that case.
    29	- **A04.** Amendment 9 does not say which receipts V1 protects after an attacker obtains authority-record
    30	  credentials. The author must decide this item.
    31	  - The recommended text protects the frames and signatures that the service keys made honestly. This protection
    32	    applies before and after the capture.
    33	  - V1 does not prevent new receipts under the attacker's own keys.
    34	  - The service must publish its authority record. A third party can inspect a substituted key if that record is
    35	    public. The verifier does not check publication. V1 does not guarantee that an attacker publishes the record.
    36	- **A05.** Amendment 9 does not give the order of the steps that select a Bitcoin attestation. The correction
    37	  gives the steps that the profile already uses.
    38	- **A06.** DNS (the Domain Name System) holds one authority record. Amendment 9 says "owner name D". The verifier
    39	  must check the DNS owner name `_tessera-v1.wamason.com`. The correction names it.
    40	
    41	Each correction has a kind:
    42	
    43	- CLARIFY: the meaning does not change.
    44	- NARROW-CLAIM: the claim becomes smaller, so that it states only what the checks establish.
    45	- CORRECT: the wording disagreed with a registered rule or model. The correction follows that rule or model
    46	  without reducing the claim.
    47	
    48	---
    49	
    50	**Provenance labels** (as in A9). **AUTHOR DECISION**: the author ruled, in his own quoted words, dated. **OWNER
    51	DECISION**: taken by the owner instance under the project ownership the author assigned on 2026-10-12; not an
    52	author ruling. **RECOMMENDED**: drafted, not decided by anyone. What the signature means for OWNER items is as in
    53	A9: the author has read them and does not object.
    54	
    55	**Weakening (A1 §A1.1).** No A1.2 property, registered check, verdict, reason code, parameter (δ, ε, k, N, S),
    56	vector, encoding, model, or scenario-mapping verdict, fixture or safe-decision category changes. A10.1–A10.4
    57	narrow sentences of A9's prose claim to what A9's own checks, A8.3's runs and the cited models establish; no
    58	evidence is withdrawn. A10.3 removes one boundary case from the claim that the registered assumption (A1.6) and the
    59	model (P5c) never covered. This signed amendment is the record A1 §A1.1 requires for each narrowing.
    60	
    61	---
    62	
    63	## A10.1 "Existed no later than T" is chain time, not UTC — OWNER DECISION, NARROW-CLAIM (review A01)
    64	
    65	A9 says the bytes and signatures "existed no later than a time T fixed by Bitcoin" (A9:36-37; again at :114,
    66	:133 and in C5, :144). A9 also says that header times are chain-time proxies (C5) and that a finite UTC cutoff
    67	"requires an additional, explicit backward-skew assumption that V1 does not establish" (:444-446). The ordinary
    68	reading of "no later than T" is a real-time bound, which A9 does not support.
    69	
    70	**Reading adopted.** "No later than T" means chain order: the bytes and exact signatures existed before the
    71	selected Bitcoin block was mined, because that block commits to their SHA-256 hash (under the registered
    72	cryptographic and chain assumptions). T is that block's header time, a chain-time value. It is not an upper bound
    73	in UTC: A2.1 registers no finite bound on B − C, and neither ε nor S is one. A UTC reading needs the additional
    74	backward-skew assumption that V1 does not establish.
    75	
    76	**Other registered uses of the same bound.** The scenario mapping (:177, :186), the issue order (:294-295) and
    77	guarantee 1 (:433-439) use T as the same clock bound. Each is qualified the same way (rows 5–9 of A10.7). These
    78	qualifications change the explanation of the safe decisions, not their verdicts, fixtures or case membership.
    79	Scenario i's parties' signature-checking duty and guarantee 1's `historical_security: NOT_EVALUATED` stay. The
    80	issue-order case is concrete: a signature made after a backward-skewed header's nominal UTC time, but before the
    81	block is mined, can fit the block's anchor.
    82	
    83	**Assembled C5 claim cell** (rows 4 and 17 of A10.7 both point here):
    84	
    85	> The frames **and their exact signatures** existed before the selected Bitcoin block was mined. T is that block's
    86	> header time, not a UTC upper bound. The selected block belongs to the qualifying Bitcoin attestation at the lowest
    87	> block height in the presented OTS proof, under A10.5's stages; T and its confirmations come from that same
    88	> attestation. The three-conjunct predicate holds at k = 6. Header times need not be monotone; selection uses chain
    89	> order, not the least header time (OWNER DECISION 2026-10-14, F03).
    90	
    91	C5's non-claims stay, with the reorganization residual amended by A10.3 (row 14).
    92	
    93	**A1.6.** A1.6 (:485-505) contains older, compressed clock and depth language. A1 stays standing. This amendment is
    94	a V1 qualification of that inherited clock wording; the unchanged historical prose does not itself establish a UTC
    95	bound. The changed sentences are rows 1–9 of A10.7.
    96	
    97	## A10.2 The window establishes two comparisons, not issuance "inside" it — OWNER DECISION, NARROW-CLAIM (review A02)
    98	
    99	A9's opening says the keys "were allowed to issue during a stated window" (:36), and guarantee 2's heading says
   100	"Signing capability mints only inside the window, in chain time" (:441). The C4 verifier rule (:419-421) checks
   101	only two comparisons, and says "T itself is not compared with not-before".
   102	
   103	**Reading adopted.** The verifier establishes exactly two inclusive comparisons, with no margin:
   104	- the declared issue time is not earlier than `issuance-not-before`;
   105	- the verified chain-time T is not later than `issuance-not-after`.
   106	
   107	It does not establish that T is not earlier than not-before, that the declared issue time is not later than
   108	not-after, or that any signing act occurred inside the interval. The review's two arithmetic witnesses satisfy the
   109	two window comparisons and all three A2 temporal comparisons. They are not executed receipt vectors or evidence
   110	that an honest issuer ships those values. The checks do not change. The issuer invariant (:422, an attempt whose T
   111	would exceed not-after is not shipped) stays an issuer duty, not a verifier comparison. "Issuance window" stays the
   112	name of the record's two values. The changed sentences are rows 10–12 of A10.7.
   113	
   114	## A10.3 Reorganization depth, at k = 6 and `DepthK = k − 1` — OWNER DECISION, NARROW-CLAIM (review A03)
   115	
   116	A9 excludes "Bitcoin history deeper than k" (:49-50) and calls "Reorganization deeper than k" a residual (C5,
   117	:144). A9 also imports k = 6 under `DepthK = k − 1` (:105) and A2.1's count, in which the including block is the
   118	first confirmation.
   119	
   120	**Definition.** The depth of a reorganization is the number of blocks it removes from the tip of the chain. A
   121	reorganization of depth d removes every block that has d or fewer confirmations when it occurs. At k = 6
   122	confirmations, five blocks follow the anchor block; P5c's `depth` counts those five (`depth = c − 1`, so
   123	`DepthK = 5`).
   124	
   125	**Correction.** The registered assumption is that anchors with k or more confirmations are permanent (A1.6:
   126	"anchors at depth ≥ k are treated as permanent"). P5c's `Reorg` action can orphan an anchor only while
   127	`depth < DepthK`, that is, at five or fewer confirmations. So a reorganization of depth exactly k, which removes an
   128	anchor with k confirmations, is outside both. A9's "deeper than k" read literally leaves that case inside the
   129	claim, with no evidence. The excluded residual is therefore **a reorganization of depth k or more**. This narrows
   130	A9's literal prose claim at equality. It changes neither the model nor its permanence assumption, and withdraws no
   131	checked model result. This does not affect C4's chain-order gap (`g + 5 < r`), which the review found correct. The
   132	changed sentences are rows 13–14 of A10.7.
   133	
   134	## A10.4 Authority capture: honest signed material protected; publication remains an issuer duty — RECOMMENDED (owner), NARROW-CLAIM, AWAITING AUTHOR RULING (review A04)
   135	
   136	A9's adversary sentence includes "anyone who captures authority-channel credentials while signing capability stays
   137	uncompromised (A8.3's sense of capture)" (:41-42). It names no set of receipts and no object for "signing
   138	capability". A8.3 (:72-76) bounds the tested property: under capture, "a captor cannot reattribute honest frames,
   139	alter honest signer sets, or carry honest signatures onto other bytes or keys". That property concerns honest
   140	service material, not only receipts issued before disclosure; the capture fixtures disclose the credentials before
   141	the honest signing processes run. A8.5 (:123-124) puts "preventing a captured or merged authority from issuing new
   142	attestations" out of scope. A captor holding both required channel credentials can sign and anchor a key assertion
   143	that names its own keys, without publishing it; receipts under those keys can then pass V1. Capture of only one
   144	required channel does not generally suffice.
   145	
   146	**The author's standard** (2026-10-08, `docs/guarantee-tiers-DRAFT.md`, "Authority capture"): *"perhaps the right
   147	way to attack the problem is distribution and admission that governance changes are out of scope"*, and
   148	*"Authority capture must be visible in such instances. For an adjudicator to be convinced incorrectly requires the
   149	takeover be invisible."* The owner summarizes it as: prevent, no; rewrite the past, no; visible, yes. The summary
   150	is the owner's, not a quotation. These words are the motivation for A10.4. They are not evidence that the visibility
   151	property is established.
   152	
   153	**Recommended text.** Replace the adversary clause with:
   154	
   155	> anyone who captures authority-channel credentials (the DNS credential or the repository key, including both
